Wooden Gaze

Ingredients: DREAM POP & SHOEGAZE + MELANCHOLIC, NOSTALGIC & SAD + Xylophone  
Creative Catalyst: The genre started as forbidden lullabies sung by androids to their unfinished siblings.

Sound Profile
Wooden Gaze sounds like a memory dissolving in warm milk. It submerges the colossal, earth-shaking blur of classic shoegaze guitars into a pool of soft, pitch-warped dream pop reveries, anchored by the crystalline, woody drop of a mic’d xylophone. Instead of relying on typical synth pads to fill the high-end frequency spectrum, the genre uses heavy delay units to stretch wooden mallet percussion into continuous, shimmering tides of melody. Vocals are buried, hushed, and double-tracked in quiet unison, creating a claustrophobic intimacy that feels like someone breathing directly into your ear under a heavy blanket.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 62–78 BPM. Rhythms are sluggish, unhurried, and slightly dragging behind the beat, utilizing brushed snare wires and muted kick drums.
- Harmonic Palette: Major 7th chords dropping into weeping minor 6ths, heavily reliant on Lydian modes to evoke an eerie, unfinished sense of childhood nostalgia.
- Production Signatures: "Mallet-Drying" (running xylophones through high-gain tape echo until the transient turns into a humming pad), "Wet-Blanket Tracking" (singing through thick woolen scarves to muffle sibilance), and extreme reverse-reverb wall-of-sound guitar processing.
- Dynamics: Songs begin in hyper-quiet, dry, acoustic solitude before swelling unpredictably into massive, oceanic walls of distorted feedback that suddenly collapse back into a single, dry xylophone clack.

Scene & Origin Lore
The style coalesced in the damp, subterranean basement venues of Leeds during the mid-2010s before spreading through obscure tape-trading forums. The subculture’s mythos traces back to a localized urban legend about early industrial assembly line prototypes designed with vocal synthesizers, programmed to hum soothing nursery rhymes to inert, half-assembled chassis in the quiet dark between factory shifts. Scene kids adopted this concept as an aesthetic armor, wearing oversized vintage kitchen aprons, faded corduroy overalls, and heavy wool sweaters worn threadbare at the elbows. Shows are performed in near-total darkness, illuminated only by incandescent bulb strips resting on the floor, while audience members sit cross-legged on worn rugs, drinking cold-brewed oat milk and listening in somber silence.

Central Instrumentation
- Rosewood Xylophone: Miked with vintage ribbon mics, pitched down, and fed through chorus pedals.
- Fender Jazzmaster: Run through three stacked fuzz pedals into reverse-delay units.
- Roland RE-201 Space Echo: Used to turn discrete mallet hits into lingering, spectral reverb tails.
- Damped Upright Piano: Played with felt wedged permanently between the hammers and strings.

For Fans Of: Cocteau Twins, Slowdive, Steve Reich, Grouper

